1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:06,489
In 2020, Tesla finally did something it had never done before: it made a profit for

2
00:00:06,489 --> 00:00:07,384
a full year.

3
00:00:07,384 --> 00:00:10,740
Seven hundred and twenty-one million dollars.

4
00:00:11,190 --> 00:00:15,973
That same year, it collected one billion, five hundred and eighty million dollars

5
00:00:15,973 --> 00:00:19,870
from a source that has nothing to do with selling cars to drivers.

6
00:00:20,320 --> 00:00:22,769
The money came from other carmakers.

7
00:00:22,769 --> 00:00:28,347
Companies like Stellantis, Toyota, Ford and GM, paying Tesla, year after year, for

8
00:00:28,347 --> 00:00:31,680
something that cost Tesla almost nothing to make.

9
00:00:32,130 --> 00:00:37,358
So the most valuable car company in the world became profitable thanks to cheques

10
00:00:37,358 --> 00:00:38,778
written by its rivals.

11
00:00:38,778 --> 00:00:42,650
And that's only the first strange thing in Tesla's accounts.

12
00:00:43,100 --> 00:00:45,980
Let's open them up, and follow every dollar.

13
00:00:49,580 --> 00:00:52,667
First, a reminder of where Tesla comes from.

14
00:00:52,667 --> 00:00:55,193
For most of its life, it lost money.

15
00:00:55,193 --> 00:01:00,946
Between 2016 and 2019 alone, its operations lost more than two point seven billion

16
00:01:00,946 --> 00:01:01,507
dollars.

17
00:01:01,507 --> 00:01:02,700
Profit is recent.

18
00:01:03,150 --> 00:01:08,428
When most people think about Tesla, they think about one thing: electric cars.

19
00:01:08,428 --> 00:01:12,150
But the company actually reports four kinds of revenue.

20
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:18,200
In 2025, Tesla brought in about ninety-five billion dollars.

21
00:01:18,650 --> 00:01:24,365
Around seventy billion came from cars: selling them, leasing them, and that strange

22
00:01:24,365 --> 00:01:28,290
line called "regulatory credits" that we'll come back to.

23
00:01:28,740 --> 00:01:35,156
Almost thirteen billion came from energy: giant batteries for power grids, home

24
00:01:35,156 --> 00:01:36,780
batteries and solar.

25
00:01:37,230 --> 00:01:43,643
And about twelve and a half billion came from what Tesla calls "services and other":

26
00:01:43,643 --> 00:01:48,910
charging at its Supercharger stations, repairs, insurance, used cars.

27
00:01:49,360 --> 00:01:53,640
Ninety-five billion dollars sounds enormous, and it is.

28
00:01:53,640 --> 00:01:59,789
But 2025 was also the first year in Tesla's history when its revenue went down.

29
00:01:59,789 --> 00:02:03,680
To understand why, we need to start with the cars.

30
00:02:04,780 --> 00:02:09,180
For a decade, Tesla's story was simple: every year, more cars.

31
00:02:09,630 --> 00:02:12,708
Half a million deliveries in 2020.

32
00:02:12,708 --> 00:02:16,510
More than one point eight million in 2023.

33
00:02:16,960 --> 00:02:18,610
Then the curve bent.

34
00:02:18,610 --> 00:02:21,664
One point seven nine million in 2024.

35
00:02:21,664 --> 00:02:26,120
One point six four million in 2025, down nine percent.

36
00:02:26,570 --> 00:02:29,140
Part of the reason is competition.

37
00:02:29,140 --> 00:02:34,883
In 2025, the Chinese company BYD sold about two point two five million fully

38
00:02:34,883 --> 00:02:41,232
electric cars, and took the title of the world's biggest seller of electric vehicles

39
00:02:41,232 --> 00:02:43,650
away from Tesla for a full year.

40
00:02:44,100 --> 00:02:46,799
There's also the question of the lineup.

41
00:02:46,799 --> 00:02:50,376
Tesla's sales now rest almost entirely on two models.

42
00:02:50,376 --> 00:02:55,639
In the third quarter of 2026, the Model 3 and Model Y made up four hundred and

43
00:02:55,639 --> 00:03:00,903
seventy-eight thousand of its four hundred and eighty-six thousand deliveries.

44
00:03:00,903 --> 00:03:06,368
Everything else, including the Cybertruck and the Model S and X, sold about eight

45
00:03:06,368 --> 00:03:09,540
thousand units, half as many as a year earlier.

46
00:03:09,990 --> 00:03:14,069
To keep selling, Tesla cut its prices, again and again.

47
00:03:14,069 --> 00:03:19,927
You can see the effect in one number: gross margin, the share of each sale left

48
00:03:19,927 --> 00:03:23,190
after paying for parts, labor and factories.

49
00:03:23,640 --> 00:03:30,407
In 2022, Tesla kept about twenty-six cents of gross profit for every dollar of

50
00:03:30,407 --> 00:03:31,101
revenue.

51
00:03:31,101 --> 00:03:35,960
By 2024 and 2025, that was down to about eighteen cents.

52
00:03:36,410 --> 00:03:38,833
That's still better than many carmakers.

53
00:03:38,833 --> 00:03:43,072
But it means each car sold brings in a lot less money than it used to.

54
00:03:43,072 --> 00:03:47,130
And it makes that strange line in the accounts even more important.

55
00:03:48,230 --> 00:03:49,390
Here's how it works.

56
00:03:49,840 --> 00:03:55,152
Governments, in places like California, the European Union and China, set limits on

57
00:03:55,152 --> 00:04:00,080
how much their cars can pollute, on average, across a carmaker's whole range.

58
00:04:00,530 --> 00:04:04,836
A company that sells lots of big gasoline vehicles may go over the limit.

59
00:04:04,836 --> 00:04:09,318
It then has two options: pay a penalty, or buy credits from a company that's

60
00:04:09,318 --> 00:04:10,970
comfortably under the limit.

61
00:04:11,420 --> 00:04:16,287
And no one is more comfortably under the limit than a company that only sells

62
00:04:16,287 --> 00:04:17,172
electric cars.

63
00:04:17,172 --> 00:04:19,384
Every Tesla sold generates credits.

64
00:04:19,384 --> 00:04:21,660
Tesla sells them to its competitors.

65
00:04:22,110 --> 00:04:27,165
The key point: producing those credits costs Tesla almost nothing.

66
00:04:27,165 --> 00:04:30,076
The cars were going to be sold anyway.

67
00:04:30,076 --> 00:04:36,510
So this revenue is, in the words of one industry newspaper, "virtually pure profit".

68
00:04:36,960 --> 00:04:38,976
The amounts grew fast.

69
00:04:38,976 --> 00:04:43,007
About three hundred million dollars in 2016.

70
00:04:43,007 --> 00:04:46,397
One point five eight billion in 2020.

71
00:04:46,397 --> 00:04:50,520
A record two point seven six billion in 2024.

72
00:04:50,970 --> 00:04:58,530
Added up since 2009, Tesla has collected almost fourteen billion dollars this way.

73
00:04:58,980 --> 00:05:01,162
Now put that next to its profits.

74
00:05:01,162 --> 00:05:06,451
In 2020, the credits were more than twice the size of Tesla's entire net profit.

75
00:05:06,451 --> 00:05:11,741
In 2025, Tesla's credits brought in around two billion dollars, almost all of it

76
00:05:11,741 --> 00:05:16,700
profit, while the company's total net income was three point eight billion.

77
00:05:17,150 --> 00:05:23,757
So in its first profitable year, and again in 2025, a big share of what Tesla kept

78
00:05:23,757 --> 00:05:28,350
came, indirectly, from other carmakers' gasoline engines.

79
00:05:29,450 --> 00:05:33,090
That source of money is now drying up, at least in the United States.

80
00:05:33,540 --> 00:05:39,794
In July 2025, a new law in the US, nicknamed the "One Big Beautiful Bill", cut back

81
00:05:39,794 --> 00:05:42,055
support for electric vehicles.

82
00:05:42,055 --> 00:05:48,159
The seven thousand five hundred dollar federal tax credit for buying one ended on

83
00:05:48,159 --> 00:05:50,118
September thirtieth, 2025.

84
00:05:50,118 --> 00:05:55,468
And the federal regulator stopped enforcing its fuel economy penalties.

85
00:05:55,468 --> 00:06:00,140
When nobody has to pay a penalty, nobody needs to buy credits.

86
00:06:00,590 --> 00:06:03,627
The result shows up in Tesla's own numbers.

87
00:06:03,627 --> 00:06:09,206
In the second quarter of 2026, its credit revenue was one hundred and forty-six

88
00:06:09,206 --> 00:06:10,335
million dollars.

89
00:06:10,335 --> 00:06:14,573
A year earlier, it was four hundred and thirty-nine million.

90
00:06:14,573 --> 00:06:16,550
That's a drop of two-thirds.

91
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:20,514
The end of the tax credit also distorted car sales.

92
00:06:20,514 --> 00:06:26,027
American buyers rushed to get their seven and a half thousand dollars before the

93
00:06:26,027 --> 00:06:31,402
deadline, which made the third quarter of 2025 Tesla's best quarter ever: four

94
00:06:31,402 --> 00:06:34,090
hundred and ninety-seven thousand cars.

95
00:06:34,090 --> 00:06:39,120
A year later, without that rush, deliveries were about two percent lower.

96
00:06:39,570 --> 00:06:43,526
Credits are still sold in other markets, like Europe and China.

97
00:06:43,526 --> 00:06:47,168
But one of Tesla's easiest sources of profit is shrinking.

98
00:06:47,168 --> 00:06:50,810
Which raises the obvious question: what's growing instead?

99
00:06:51,910 --> 00:06:53,310
The answer isn't a car.

100
00:06:53,760 --> 00:06:59,322
Tesla builds huge battery systems, each one about the size of a shipping container,

101
00:06:59,322 --> 00:07:04,617
that power companies use to store electricity and release it when demand peaks.

102
00:07:04,617 --> 00:07:06,560
It also sells home batteries.

103
00:07:07,010 --> 00:07:12,645
In 2020, Tesla installed about three gigawatt-hours of storage.

104
00:07:12,645 --> 00:07:15,328
In 2024, more than thirty-one.

105
00:07:15,328 --> 00:07:17,832
In 2025, almost forty-seven.

106
00:07:17,832 --> 00:07:21,410
That's fifteen times more in five years.

107
00:07:21,860 --> 00:07:28,524
Revenue followed: from two billion dollars in 2020 to almost thirteen billion in

108
00:07:28,524 --> 00:07:28,940
2025.

109
00:07:29,390 --> 00:07:32,594
And this business is more profitable than the cars.

110
00:07:32,594 --> 00:07:37,810
In 2024, Tesla kept about twenty-six cents of gross profit on each dollar of energy

111
00:07:37,810 --> 00:07:42,836
sales, and close to thirty cents in 2025, compared with roughly eighteen for the

112
00:07:42,836 --> 00:07:44,030
company as a whole.

113
00:07:44,480 --> 00:07:47,401
It's not the part of Tesla that makes headlines.

114
00:07:47,401 --> 00:07:50,200
But it's the part that's been growing fastest.

115
00:07:51,300 --> 00:07:53,380
The third business is quieter still.

116
00:07:53,830 --> 00:07:59,907
By the end of 2024, Tesla operated almost seven thousand Supercharger stations, with

117
00:07:59,907 --> 00:08:03,524
more than sixty-five thousand charging connectors.

118
00:08:03,524 --> 00:08:06,851
Every time a driver plugs in, Tesla gets paid.

119
00:08:06,851 --> 00:08:12,639
Add repairs, insurance, parts and used cars, and "services and other" brought in

120
00:08:12,639 --> 00:08:17,630
about twelve and a half billion dollars in 2025, up nineteen percent.

121
00:08:18,080 --> 00:08:24,082
The more Teslas there are on the road, the more this line grows, even in a year when

122
00:08:24,082 --> 00:08:25,440
new car sales fall.

123
00:08:26,540 --> 00:08:28,459
So that's where the money comes from.

124
00:08:28,459 --> 00:08:29,340
Where does it go?

125
00:08:29,790 --> 00:08:34,771
First, into building cars and batteries: after those costs, only about eighteen

126
00:08:34,771 --> 00:08:37,482
percent of revenue is left as gross profit.

127
00:08:37,482 --> 00:08:42,588
Then into research, salaries, sales and administration for a company of about one

128
00:08:42,588 --> 00:08:45,110
hundred and thirty-five thousand people.

129
00:08:45,560 --> 00:08:48,154
What's left has been shrinking.

130
00:08:48,154 --> 00:08:54,850
Leaving aside a one-time tax benefit in 2023, Tesla's net profit peaked at about

131
00:08:54,850 --> 00:08:58,365
twelve and a half billion dollars in 2022.

132
00:08:58,365 --> 00:09:01,880
In 2025, it was three point eight billion.

133
00:09:02,330 --> 00:09:07,964
Meanwhile, spending on new factories and equipment kept climbing: three point two

134
00:09:07,964 --> 00:09:12,138
billion dollars in 2020, eleven point three billion in 2024.

135
00:09:12,138 --> 00:09:17,841
In 2024, after paying for all of it, Tesla still had about three point six billion

136
00:09:17,841 --> 00:09:19,650
dollars of free cash flow.

137
00:09:20,100 --> 00:09:24,511
And Tesla is now spending even more heavily on its next bets.

138
00:09:24,511 --> 00:09:30,441
In the second quarter of 2026 alone, its capital spending reached five point eight

139
00:09:30,441 --> 00:09:31,598
billion dollars.

140
00:09:31,598 --> 00:09:37,094
That quarter, its free cash flow, the cash left after those investments, was

141
00:09:37,094 --> 00:09:40,420
negative: minus one point one billion dollars.

142
00:09:40,870 --> 00:09:45,770
Revenue that quarter was actually a record: twenty-eight billion dollars, up

143
00:09:45,770 --> 00:09:46,995
twenty-six percent.

144
00:09:46,995 --> 00:09:52,152
But net profit was one point one billion, and more than half of that came from a

145
00:09:52,152 --> 00:09:57,374
five-hundred-and-ninety-million-dollar gain outside its normal business, not from

146
00:09:57,374 --> 00:09:58,470
selling anything.

147
00:09:59,570 --> 00:10:04,610
If you only looked at these numbers, you'd see a large, profitable carmaker, with

148
00:10:04,610 --> 00:10:07,970
slowing car sales and a fast-growing battery business.

149
00:10:08,420 --> 00:10:10,700
That's not how Tesla's shareholders see it.

150
00:10:11,150 --> 00:10:16,859
In November 2025, more than seventy-five percent of them voted for a new pay plan

151
00:10:16,859 --> 00:10:22,003
for Elon Musk, potentially worth up to a trillion dollars over ten years.

152
00:10:22,003 --> 00:10:27,853
It was opposed by Norway's sovereign wealth fund and by the two biggest shareholder

153
00:10:27,853 --> 00:10:28,910
advisory firms.

154
00:10:29,360 --> 00:10:33,746
What matters for us is what Musk has to deliver to get paid.

155
00:10:33,746 --> 00:10:38,278
The plan lists the targets: twenty million vehicles delivered.

156
00:10:38,278 --> 00:10:42,956
Ten million subscriptions to Tesla's driver-assistance software.

157
00:10:42,956 --> 00:10:45,002
One million humanoid robots.

158
00:10:45,002 --> 00:10:48,219
One million robotaxis in commercial service.

159
00:10:48,219 --> 00:10:49,388
A profit target.

160
00:10:49,388 --> 00:10:53,920
And a stock market value of eight and a half trillion dollars.

161
00:10:54,370 --> 00:10:56,068
Look at that list again.

162
00:10:56,068 --> 00:10:58,475
Cars are only one item out of six.

163
00:10:58,475 --> 00:11:04,348
Three of them are about software and machines that, today, bring in little revenue:

164
00:11:04,348 --> 00:11:08,170
driver-assistance subscriptions, robotaxis and robots.

165
00:11:08,620 --> 00:11:13,121
In other words, the shareholders who approved this plan aren't mainly paying for the

166
00:11:13,121 --> 00:11:14,461
Tesla we just took apart.

167
00:11:14,461 --> 00:11:17,140
They're paying for a Tesla that doesn't exist yet.

168
00:11:18,240 --> 00:11:20,480
So, how does Tesla actually make money?

169
00:11:20,930 --> 00:11:26,054
Mostly by selling cars, at thinner margins than before, in a market where it's no

170
00:11:26,054 --> 00:11:29,028
longer the biggest seller of electric vehicles.

171
00:11:29,028 --> 00:11:32,444
Increasingly, by selling batteries to power companies.

172
00:11:32,444 --> 00:11:34,975
Steadily, through charging and services.

173
00:11:34,975 --> 00:11:40,226
And, for years, thanks to billions of dollars paid by its own competitors, a source

174
00:11:40,226 --> 00:11:42,250
of profit that is now shrinking.

175
00:11:42,700 --> 00:11:46,973
Whether Tesla is worth what investors pay for it depends on something that doesn't

176
00:11:46,973 --> 00:11:48,484
appear in these accounts yet.

177
00:11:48,484 --> 00:11:50,777
That's not a question we can answer for you.

178
00:11:50,777 --> 00:11:54,060
But now you know exactly what's in the numbers, and what isn't.
